00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:16.479
So a large language model such as GPT of
course it is something which is designed

00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:21.920
to predict what text what words should
come after an input. You give it an

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:27.920
input sequence of words and it says what
will come next. It's like autocomplete

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:32.880
on steroids. And of course we know that
the way it actually works is it's

00:01:29.439 --> 00:01:38.799
basically this enormous pattern matching
statistical engine. It's been fed tons

00:01:35.759 --> 00:01:43.439
and tons of training data, example data
from the internet and far beyond. And

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:47.680
it's looked at all of this data. It's
learned patterns in the data. And so,

00:01:45.759 --> 00:01:52.640
it's got really good when it's given
some input text at predicting what will

00:01:50.399 --> 00:01:57.840
come next. And of course, I used the
word words there, but it's really what

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:02.079
we call tokens. the input is actually a
sequence of tokens where a token is a

00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:07.040
kind of chunk of a few letters that
often is a word but is often also a part

00:02:04.799 --> 00:02:13.440
of a word. And so there's a sequence of
these input tokens and the output from

00:02:10.319 --> 00:02:17.520
an LLM is not necessarily the the most
likely next token. We often say it that

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:24.319
way, but what it actually gives you is
the probability of every possible next

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:29.760
token. If the input is 2 + 2 is then the
output there's a very high probability

00:02:26.319 --> 00:02:34.160
that the output is the token for four.
But there might also be a probability of

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:39.920
other words like the word bananas but
presumably that would be a very low

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:43.360
probability. 2 + 2 is bananas is not a
very common thing to say. It won't have

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:48.160
appeared very often in the training
data. But that's the idea. The input is

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:54.319
a sequence of these things called tokens
and the output is the probability of the

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:58.400
next token. That's what an LLM does. And
I imagine you're familiar with this. But

00:02:56.239 --> 00:03:04.000
of course, one of the ways that LLMs
work is that they generate the output

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:09.280
one token at a time. So if the input is
something like what is the capital of

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:14.000
France, then the first word, the first
token that it predicts to come next

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:19.760
might be the word the. And then you'd
pass in what is the capital of France?

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:24.319
The And the next token might be capital.
And then what is the capital of France?

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:31.440
The capital. And then what is the
capital of France? The capital of France

00:03:27.599 --> 00:03:36.000
is and then Paris. And so it generates
things one token at a time. And the

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:40.640
whole input is passed back in with all
the tokens so far. And then it predicts

00:03:38.319 --> 00:03:44.959
the next token. And that is the way that
LLMs are run. That process is called

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:49.360
inference and they're run that way to
generate the output one token at a time.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:52.959
And if you want to know more about that,
I have a sort of YouTube series that

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:57.040
goes through this, a playlist that I
will include in the course resources. I

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:01.760
imagine you know this already, but it's
good that we're on the same page. Okay,

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:06.400
that's an LLM. And then also so we're on
the same page, there is something

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:12.560
different but confusingly sounding
similar called an like an AI application

00:04:09.439 --> 00:04:17.600
or an AI product. And this is something
that uses an LLM to achieve a business

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:22.400
goal, but it's got software written
around that call to an LLM. And the

00:04:20.479 --> 00:04:25.520
distinction I'm trying to make here is
that something like there's a there's an

00:04:23.919 --> 00:04:30.639
LLM that's very well known that's called
GPT that I'm sure you're familiar with.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:35.759
GPT is an LLM. It's something which is a
model that predicts next tokens and then

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:42.080
there is an AI application that's called
chat GPT and that is a piece of software

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:46.160
that wraps around calling GPT and it has
other functionality. It has things like

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:53.840
a memory and the ability to search the
web and other stuff built around using

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:59.280
the GPT LLM and chat GPT then is a
classic example. The cursor agent that

00:04:56.479 --> 00:05:03.680
we used yesterday is another example. If
you use Dualingo to learn a new

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:08.560
language, the Dualingo Max plan has a
premium feature that lets you chat with

00:05:05.759 --> 00:05:13.360
an AI in order to learn a language.
That's another one. You may be familiar

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:17.680
with Atlassian Robo, which is like Genai
built into the Atlassian suite of

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:22.000
products. There's so many different
places where you will find Genai being

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:26.320
packaged into an application. And the
the reason that I draw attention to this

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:30.400
and and all of the many others is just
to draw that distinction between a

00:05:27.919 --> 00:05:37.600
software product, an application that
uses an LLM from the LLM itself. Keep in

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:43.759
your mind separate chat GBT the product
from GPT the LLM which can be called to

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:50.479
generate the most likely next token to
follow a sequence. And that GPT LLM sits

00:05:47.199 --> 00:05:56.560
behind the chat GPT product. And ever
since the release of Chat GPT in 2022,

00:05:53.759 --> 00:06:02.639
we've all been a bit surprised that
autocomplete, basically predictive text

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:08.080
on steroids, is able to show this
apparent intelligence. It's able to do

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:13.120
things like we saw the cursor agent do,
and it's able to be like like chat GPT.

00:06:10.639 --> 00:06:18.080
It's it's able to have an informed
conversation with us. It's it's very

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:22.960
very surprising. And there are a few
tricks that underly this. And in

00:06:20.319 --> 00:06:26.000
particular, there are four tricks that I
want to highlight that are that are

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:31.520
written in software. They're sort of
built around this call to this thing

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:35.199
that just predicts likely next tokens.
And let me quickly go through four of

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:39.199
these tricks with you now. And the first
trick, which is one again I imagine most

00:06:37.199 --> 00:06:44.319
of you are super clear on already, but
let's get on the same page. I call it

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:49.919
the illusion of memory. And it's
recognizing this very simple point which

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:57.120
is that every time that you use an LLM
like GPT from OpenAI, it is completely

00:06:54.080 --> 00:07:01.120
stateless. GPT is a model, a data
science model that takes an input

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:05.440
sequence of tokens and it predicts what
will follow that input sequence and it's

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:10.800
completely stateless in that every time
you call it with an input sequence, it's

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:15.919
it's got no knowledge of previous calls.
Every call is a separate call. And so if

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:22.319
you write some code that just uses the
GPT model and you say, "I'm Ed." And it

00:07:19.599 --> 00:07:27.520
replies, "Hi, Ed." And then you send
another call to the LLM, you you then

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:32.000
use the same LLM to put in a new input
sequence, which is, "Who am I?" It's

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:34.960
going to respond with something like, "I
don't know. I don't have that

00:07:33.199 --> 00:07:39.680
information." That's the most likely
text to come after, "Who am I?" But

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:44.160
wait, you say that that doesn't match my
experience. I I use chat GPT and for

00:07:42.319 --> 00:07:49.440
sure it keeps the conversation thread
going. If I say uh who am I? It will say

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:53.759
you're Ed. You just told me. Okay. Well,
you probably know this, but but that is

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:58.800
because of this trick, this sneaky
little trick, which is of course that

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:04.080
every time you call an LLM, you don't
just pass in something like who am I?

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:09.759
You pass in the complete conversation so
far. So what actually happens with chat

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:15.759
GPT when you're using the product chat
GPT is that you start by saying hi Ed

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:22.560
and it responds hello Ed and then the
next message that gets sent to GPT is

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:28.080
the whole conversation so far I'm Ed hi
Ed who am I the next thing I've typed

00:08:25.599 --> 00:08:33.519
and when that entire input sequence is
sent in of course the LLM predicts the

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:38.880
most likely next token is something like
you're red you just told me you're at.

00:08:35.839 --> 00:08:44.720
And that gives this illusion that chat
GPT has a memory. It is in fact just a

00:08:42.159 --> 00:08:50.240
byproduct of every single time the LLM
is called, the entire conversation so

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:54.560
far is passed in. Most of you knew that
already. And if you didn't, you're like,

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:58.560
"Oh, I see." And if you do something
like my my uh AI engineering core track,

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:02.080
we actually make these calls and you see
it happening just in case you don't

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:04.959
believe me. And the second trick that
again people are pretty familiar with at

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:09.680
this point is this trick called
reasoning or thinking. And this began

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:14.480
out as this this thing we discovered
where when you were putting in this

00:09:11.519 --> 00:09:20.080
input sequence to GPT if you put at the
end of the input sequence please think

00:09:16.959 --> 00:09:24.880
step by step you tended to get better
outcomes which was sort of surprising

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:29.680
and that led over time to this different
way of working with LLMs in which they

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:33.360
were trained before they give you the
answer to output the way in which

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:38.880
they're going to come up with the
answer. And it's very strange, but it

00:09:36.399 --> 00:09:44.800
turns out that as a side effect of
generating tokens to describe what it's

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:50.640
going to do, you end up getting better
outcomes. And that sounds it just sounds

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:54.880
hokey. It sounds too good to be true.
And yet it works. And here's a sort of

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:59.519
classic example. Again, I do this on my
AI engineering course. You could have

00:09:56.959 --> 00:10:04.399
the question, hey, you toss two coins,
one of them is heads. What's the chances

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:08.720
the other one is tails? And I show that
when you run this with a model without

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:13.519
it outputting tokens to give its
reasoning, it will give an answer like a

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:17.040
half 50/50, which is what you might
instinctively think would be the right

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:21.040
answer. But in fact, it's one of those
annoying trick questions, which is a

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:24.160
clever little trick. Uh you could you
could ask chat GPT if you wanted to tell

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:29.519
you why, but the real answer is
twothirds. And if you ask it to explain

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:34.079
itself, if you if you want it to output
how it will think about this first and

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:38.560
then come up with the answer, then it
tends to get the answer right. It says

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:42.000
2/3 and it first says this is probably a
trick question. I should think this one

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:45.440
through and it realizes of course you're
in this situation where you're not

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:49.120
saying which one of the coins is heads
and that means that in twothirds of the

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:54.000
cases the other one will be tails. Look
it up. It's sneaky. But that is this

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:58.959
trick called reasoning or thinking. And
it's resulted in much stronger models

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:03.440
that are trained to generate tokens to
think through what they're going to do

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:03.440
before they do

